## The problem generics solve

A function like `first(arr)` should work for arrays of any element type and still tell the caller **which** type comes out. Without generics you choose between `any` (loses information) and duplicate functions.

```ts
function first<T>(arr: readonly T[]): T | undefined { return arr[0]; }
const n = first([1, 2, 3]);        // T inferred as number -> number | undefined
const s = first(["a", "b"]);       // string | undefined
```

`T` is a **type parameter**: a placeholder filled in at each call, usually inferred from the arguments. Generics appear on functions, interfaces, type aliases and classes: `Promise<T>`, `Array<T>`, `Map<K, V>`.

## Constraints

Restrict what a type parameter may be with `extends`:

```ts
function longest<T extends { length: number }>(a: T, b: T): T { return a.length >= b.length ? a : b; }
longest("abc", "de");           // ok: string has length
longest([1], [1, 2, 3]);        // ok: arrays
// longest(1, 2);               // error: number has no length
```

**`keyof`** gives the union of an object type's keys; combine with constraints to build type-safe property access:

```ts
function pick<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, keys: K[]): Pick<T, K> {
  const out = {} as Pick<T, K>;
  for (const k of keys) out[k] = obj[k];
  return out;
}
const u = pick({ id: 1, name: "A", pw: "x" }, ["id", "name"]);   // { id: number; name: string }
```

## Defaults, multiple parameters and inference

- Defaults: `interface Page<T = unknown> { items: T[]; next?: string }`.
- Several parameters: `function mapValues<T, R>(o: Record<string, T>, f: (v: T) => R): Record<string, R>`.
- Don't add a type parameter that appears only once: `function log<T>(x: T)` is just `(x: unknown)`. A good generic **relates** two or more positions (input and output, key and value).
- Prefer letting inference work; supply explicit type arguments only when it can't (`new Map<string, number>()`).

## Generic classes and interfaces

```ts
class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  push(x: T) { this.items.push(x); }
  pop(): T | undefined { return this.items.pop(); }
}
interface Repository<T extends { id: string }> {
  get(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  save(item: T): Promise<void>;
}
```

